### Why does JPA have an owning side and an inverse side?

In a `@OneToMany(mappedBy = "portfolio")` and `@ManyToOne` pair, the `@ManyToOne` side owns the relationship because its table holds the `portfolio_id` foreign key column. The `mappedBy` side is inverse -- Hibernate ignores it when deciding what to write to the database.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import jakarta.persistence.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

@Entity
public class Portfolio {

    @Id
    private String id;

    // INVERSE side -- mappedBy means "Holding.portfolio owns the FK"
    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "portfolio", cascade = CascadeType.ALL)
    private List<Holding> holdings = new ArrayList<>();
}

@Entity
public class Holding {

    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.SEQUENCE)
    private Long id;
    private String symbol;
    private int qty;
    private BigDecimal price;

    // OWNING side -- this table has the portfolio_id FK column
    @ManyToOne
    @JoinColumn(name = "portfolio_id")
    private Portfolio portfolio;
}
```

```text
Database tables:
portfolios: id, owner_name
holdings: id, symbol, qty, price, portfolio_id (FK)

Hibernate writes the FK based on Holding.portfolio (owning side)
Hibernate IGNORES Portfolio.holdings for persistence
```

### What goes wrong if you update only the inverse side?

If you add a Holding to the portfolio's list but never set `holding.setPortfolio(portfolio)`, the owning side's foreign key is never set. Hibernate writes `portfolio_id = null`, and the holding is not linked to the portfolio in the database.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.Holding;
import com.bank.model.Portfolio;
import jakarta.persistence.EntityManager;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class BrokenInverseSideDemo {

    private final EntityManager em;

    public BrokenInverseSideDemo(EntityManager em) {
        this.em = em;
    }

    @Transactional
    public void brokenAdd() {
        Portfolio portfolio = em.find(Portfolio.class, "PF-001");
        Holding holding = new Holding("INFY", 200, new BigDecimal("1500.00"));

        // WRONG: only inverse side updated
        portfolio.getHoldings().add(holding);
        em.persist(holding);
        // holding.portfolio is null -> portfolio_id = NULL in the database!
    }
}
```

```text
Expected SQL:
INSERT INTO holdings (symbol, qty, price, portfolio_id) VALUES ('INFY', 200, 1500, NULL)
-- portfolio_id is NULL because the owning side was never set!
-- The holding exists but is not linked to any portfolio
```

### What goes wrong if you update only the owning side?

If you set `holding.setPortfolio(portfolio)` but never add it to the portfolio's list, the database is correct but the in-memory parent list is stale. Within the same transaction, iterating `portfolio.getHoldings()` misses the new holding.

```java
@Transactional
public void brokenOwningSideOnly() {
    Portfolio portfolio = em.find(Portfolio.class, "PF-001");
    Holding holding = new Holding("TCS", 50, new BigDecimal("3200.00"));

    // WRONG: only owning side updated
    holding.setPortfolio(portfolio);
    em.persist(holding);
    // Database: portfolio_id = 'PF-001' -- correct!
    // In-memory: portfolio.getHoldings() does NOT include the new holding

    int count = portfolio.getHoldings().size();
    // count is WRONG -- misses the new holding until the context is refreshed
}
```

```text
Expected behavior:
- Database FK: portfolio_id = 'PF-001' (correct)
- In-memory list: portfolio.getHoldings() does NOT include the new holding
- Any logic that counts holdings or calculates totals is WRONG
- After the context closes and reloads, the holding appears -- confusing!
```

### How do helper methods fix this?

Helper methods on the parent update both sides atomically. Callers use `portfolio.addHolding(h)` and both views are always consistent.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import jakarta.persistence.*;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.List;

@Entity
public class Portfolio {

    @Id
    private String id;

    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "portfolio", cascade = CascadeType.ALL, orphanRemoval = true)
    private List<Holding> holdings = new ArrayList<>();

    public void addHolding(Holding h) {
        holdings.add(h);          // inverse side (in-memory list)
        h.setPortfolio(this);     // owning side (FK) -- THIS gets persisted
    }

    public void removeHolding(Holding h) {
        holdings.remove(h);       // triggers orphanRemoval
        h.setPortfolio(null);     // clear owning side
    }

    public List<Holding> getHoldings() {
        return Collections.unmodifiableList(holdings);
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.Holding;
import com.bank.model.Portfolio;
import jakarta.persistence.EntityManager;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class CorrectRelationshipService {

    private final EntityManager em;

    public CorrectRelationshipService(EntityManager em) {
        this.em = em;
    }

    @Transactional
    public void addHoldingCorrectly() {
        Portfolio portfolio = em.find(Portfolio.class, "PF-001");
        Holding holding = new Holding("HDFC", 100, new BigDecimal("1650.00"));

        portfolio.addHolding(holding); // both sides updated!
        // FK: portfolio_id = 'PF-001' (owning side set)
        // In-memory: portfolio.getHoldings() includes the new holding
    }
}
```

```text
Expected SQL:
INSERT INTO holdings (symbol, qty, price, portfolio_id) VALUES ('HDFC', 100, 1650, 'PF-001')
-- portfolio_id is correctly set because the helper method set the owning side
-- In-memory list is consistent because the helper added to the collection
```

### What problems does inconsistent state cause?

Beyond wrong FK values and stale collections, inconsistent state causes orphanRemoval to misfire, equals/hashCode to behave unexpectedly, and confusing bugs where the database differs from what the code sees.

```text
Problems from inconsistent state:
1. NULL foreign key -- holding not linked to portfolio in DB
2. Stale collection -- counts, totals, validations are wrong in the same TX
3. orphanRemoval misfire -- collection state inconsistent, wrong deletes
4. equals/hashCode issues -- Set membership misbehaves
5. Confusing reload -- after TX, reload shows different state than what code saw
```

### How do you handle @ManyToMany synchronization?

For `@ManyToMany`, both sides have collections. The helper methods must update both collections.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import jakarta.persistence.*;
import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.Set;

@Entity
public class Fund {

    @Id
    private String id;

    @ManyToMany
    @JoinTable(name = "fund_accounts",
        joinColumns = @JoinColumn(name = "fund_id"),
        inverseJoinColumns = @JoinColumn(name = "account_id"))
    private Set<Account> accounts = new HashSet<>();

    public void addAccount(Account a) {
        accounts.add(a);        // owning side
        a.getFunds().add(this); // inverse side
    }

    public void removeAccount(Account a) {
        accounts.remove(a);
        a.getFunds().remove(this);
    }
}
```

```text
Expected behavior:
- addAccount() updates both collections
- The join table row is created based on the owning side
- Both in-memory views are consistent
```

### What about equals and hashCode with bidirectional relationships?

Implement `equals()`/`hashCode()` on a stable business key, not the generated id. Before the id is assigned (pre-persist), two different entities would have the same hash (null), causing Set issues.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import jakarta.persistence.*;
import java.util.Objects;

@Entity
public class Holding {

    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.SEQUENCE)
    private Long id;
    private String symbol;

    @ManyToOne
    @JoinColumn(name = "portfolio_id")
    private Portfolio portfolio;

    // Use business key (symbol + portfolio) instead of generated id
    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object o) {
        if (this == o) return true;
        if (!(o instanceof Holding h)) return false;
        return Objects.equals(symbol, h.symbol)
            && Objects.equals(portfolio, h.portfolio);
    }

    @Override
    public int hashCode() {
        return Objects.hash(symbol);
    }
}
```

```text
Best practices for equals/hashCode:
1. Use a stable business key (e.g., symbol + portfolio)
2. Do NOT use the generated id (null before persist)
3. hashCode should use only immutable fields
4. Consistent equals/hashCode prevents Set membership bugs
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer makes the owning-side setter package-private and exposes only helper methods on the aggregate root. They return unmodifiable collection views. They implement equals/hashCode on a business key. They know that orphanRemoval only works correctly when the collection state is consistent.

```text
Senior checklist:
1. Always use helper methods for bidirectional associations
2. Make the owning-side setter package-private
3. Return unmodifiable collection views
4. Implement equals/hashCode on a business key, not the generated id
5. orphanRemoval depends on consistent collection state
6. For @ManyToMany, synchronize both collections
7. Test both add and remove paths in integration tests
```
